te`},{name:`twitter:card`,content:`summary_large_image`},{name:`twitter:title`,content:`LA Restaurant Brokerage, Consulting & Licensing | Penthouse Advisors`},{name:`twitter:description`,content:`Restaurant brokerage, consulting, licensing, permitting & ADA compliance for LA County.`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:`https://www.penthouseadvisors.com`},{rel:`preload`,as:`image`,href:gd,fetchpriority:`high`}]}),component:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./routes-DixnRCX3.js`),__vite__mapDeps([32,2,1,3,4,6])),`component`)}),vd=D(`/help/`)({head:()=>({meta:[{title:`Help Center — Restaurant Advisory Guides | Penthouse Advisors`},{name:`description`,content:`The Penthouse Advisors help center: guides and FAQs on buying, selling, leasing, licensing, and consulting for Los Angeles restaurants.`},{property:`og:title`,content:`Penthouse Advisors Help Center`},{property:`og:description`,content:`Guides and FAQs on buying, selling, leasing, and licensing Los Angeles restaurants.`},{property:`og:url`,content:`${Fe}/help`},{property:`og:type`,content:`website`},{name:`twitter:card`,content:`summary_large_image`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:`${Fe}/help`}],scripts:[Ne([{name:`Home`,path:`/`},{name:`Help Center`,path:`/help`}])]}),component:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./help.index-DUDpHk0s.js`),__vite__mapDeps([33,2,1,3,4])),`component`)}),yd=D(`/blog/`)({head:()=>({meta:[{title:`Blog: LA Restaurant Brokerage Insights | Penthouse Advisors`},{name:`description`,content:`Guides, market insight, and licensing explainers for buying, selling, and leasing restaurants in Los Angeles County, from the team at Penthouse Advisors.`},{property:`og:title`,content:`Penthouse Advisors Blog`},{property:`og:description`,content:`Guides, market insight, and licensing explainers for buying, selling, and leasing restaurants in Los Angeles.`},{property:`og:url`,content:`${Fe}/blog`},{property:`og:type`,content:`website`},{name:`twitter:card`,content:`summary_large_image`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:`${Fe}/blog`}],scripts:[Ne([{name:`Home`,path:`/`},{name:`Blog`,path:`/blog`}]),je({name:`Penthouse Advisors Blog`,description:`Guides, market insight, and licensing explainers for buying, selling, and leasing restaurants in Los Angeles County.`,path:`/blog`})]}),component:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./blog.index-BVttcYBK.js`),__vite__mapDeps([34,1,2,3,4])),`component`)}),bd=[{slug:`restaurant-valuation-los-angeles-2026`,title:`How Much Is My LA Restaurant Worth? A 2026 Valuation Guide`,seoTitle:`Restaurant Valuation Los Angeles (2026 Guide) | Penthouse Advisors`,description:`How restaurant valuation actually works in Los Angeles: the SDE multiple method, what moves your multiple up or down, and how to get a defensible number before you list.`,keywords:[`restaurant valuation Los Angeles`,`how much is my restaurant worth`,`sell my restaurant LA`,`restaurant SDE multiple`,`restaurant business appraisal Los Angeles`],category:`Selling`,publishedAt:`2026-08-27`,readMinutes:8,keyTakeaways:[`LA restaurants are valued primarily on a multiple of Seller's Discretionary Earnings (SDE), not revenue.`,`Lease term, license type, and how dependent the business is on the owner personally all move the multiple.`,`Real estate (if you own the building) is valued and sold separately from the operating business.`,`A defensible valuation takes 24 months of reconciled financials. Guessing from revenue alone undervalues most sellers.`,`Get a valuation before you make any changes to staffing, pricing, or hours: those changes affect the number.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`The short answer`,id:`the-short-answer`},{type:`p`,text:`Most independently owned Los Angeles restaurants sell for somewhere between 1.5 and 3 times Seller's Discretionary Earnings (SDE), not revenue. Where a specific restaurant lands in that range depends mainly on lease term remaining, license type, how reconciled the books are, and how much the business depends on the current owner personally. A restaurant with a fresh 10-year lease, a Type 47 license, and a GM who isn't going anywhere sits at the high end. A month-to-month lease and an owner who is the entire operation sits at the low end, regardless of how good the food is.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Why LA restaurants are valued on SDE, not revenue`,id:`sde-not-revenue`},{type:`p`,text:`Revenue tells a buyer almost nothing about what they'll actually take home. Seller's Discretionary Earnings starts with net profit, then adds back the owner's compensation, one-time or personal expenses run through the business, and non-cash items like depreciation. What's left is roughly what a new owner-operator could expect to pay themselves in year one. Buyers and their lenders price the deal off that number, so it's the number that matters when you're deciding what to list at.`},{type:`h3`,text:`What counts as an add-back`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The owner's salary and any family members on payroll who won't stay post-sale.`,`Owner health insurance, vehicle, and cell phone if run through the business.`,`One-time repairs, legal fees, or equipment purchases that won't recur.`,`Interest, depreciation, and amortization.`,`Rent paid above or below fair market value, if the owner also owns the building.`]},{type:`p`,text:`Every add-back needs a paper trail. Buyers and their accountants will ask for it, and an add-back you can't document gets thrown out of the calculation, which lowers your effective SDE and your price.`},{type:`h2`,text:`What moves your multiple up, and what pulls it down`,id:`what-moves-the-multiple`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Lease term: 5+ years remaining, with options, is worth materially more than 1–2 years left with no renewal language.`,`License type: a Type 47 (full liquor) or a rare CUB-overlay license adds value a beer-and-wine-only concept doesn't have.`,`Owner dependence: if the concept, recipes, and vendor relationships live entirely in the owner's head, buyers discount for the risk of losing all of it at closing.`,`Financial hygiene: 24 months of reconciled POS reports, bank statements, and sales-tax filings that tie together cleanly.`,`Physical condition: a hood, grease trap, and grease interceptor that pass inspection without a rebuild.`,`Trailing trend: a restaurant with flat or growing SDE over the past two years values higher than one on a declining trend, even at the same current SDE.`]},{type:`callout`,title:`If you own the real estate`,text:`The building and the operating business are two different assets with two different valuation methods. The restaurant business is valued on SDE; the real estate is valued like any commercial property, on comparable sales and cap rate. Don't let a buyer blend the two into one number. Get each appraised separately.`},{type:`h2`,text:`A simplified example`,id:`simplified-example`},{type:`p`,text:`Say a full-service restaurant in West LA nets $180,000 in SDE after add-backs, carries a Type 47 license, and has 6 years left on its lease. A broker might place that restaurant at a 2.2–2.5× multiple, putting the asking price in the $395,000–$450,000 range before inventory and any FF&E adjustments. The same $180,000 SDE with 8 months left on a lease and no license would land closer to 1.2–1.5×, purely because of the risk a buyer is taking on around continuity. The number changes with the deal terms, not just the P&L.`},{type:`h2`,text:`How to get a valuation you can actually rely on`,id:`getting-a-real-valuation`},{type:`p`,text:`A real valuation starts with 24 months of POS reports, bank deposits, merchant processor statements, and sales-tax filings, reconciled against each other. From there, a broker who works LA restaurant deals specifically, not general small-business sales, applies current local comps for your license type, neighborhood, and lease structure. That local comp knowledge is the difference between a number a buyer's lender will actually underwrite and a number that just sounds good.`}],faqs:[{q:`How much is my restaurant worth in Los Angeles?`,a:`Most independent LA restaurants sell for 1.5–3× Seller's Discretionary Earnings, adjusted for lease term, license type, and how dependent the business is on the current owner. A no-cost valuation call gets you a defensible range specific to your restaurant.`},{q:`Does revenue or profit matter more for valuation?`,a:`Profit, specifically Seller's Discretionary Earnings, is what buyers and lenders price the deal on. Two restaurants with identical revenue can have very different values if their profit margins, add-backs, or lease terms differ.`},{q:`Do I need a formal appraisal to sell my restaurant?`,a:`Not always. A broker valuation based on comparable LA sales and your reconciled financials is usually sufficient for a business sale. A formal, certified appraisal is more common when real estate is involved, financing requires one, or there's a legal need (partnership dispute, estate, divorce).`},{q:`How long is a restaurant valuation good for?`,a:`Treat it as accurate for 3–6 months. Rent increases, a lease renewal (or expiration getting closer), a change in sales trend, or a shift in the local licensing market can all move the number before then.`},{q:`Will renovating or changing my menu increase my sale price?`,a:`Sometimes, but proceed carefully before a sale: cosmetic renovations rarely pay for themselves in the final multiple, and menu or staffing changes made right before a listing can temporarily distort your trailing financials in ways that make buyers nervous rather than confident.`}],related:[{slug:`restaurant-buyer-due-diligence-checklist`,title:`The Restaurant Buyer's Due Diligence Checklist`}]},{slug:`type-41-vs-type-47-liquor-license-california`,title:`Type 41 vs. Type 47 Liquor License: What's the Difference?`,seoTitle:`Type 41 vs Type 47 Liquor License California | Penthouse Advisors`,description:`Type 41 covers beer and wine only. Type 47 covers full liquor. Here's what each California ABC license actually allows, what they cost to acquire, and which one your restaurant needs.`,keywords:[`Type 41 liquor license`,`Type 47 liquor license`,`California ABC license types`,`restaurant liquor license Los Angeles`,`beer and wine license vs full liquor license`],category:`Licensing`,publishedAt:`2026-08-27`,readMinutes:7,keyTakeaways:[`Type 41 allows on-sale beer and wine only, at a bona fide eating place.`,`Type 47 allows on-sale general (beer, wine, and distilled spirits) at a bona fide eating place.`,`Both require the location to operate primarily as a restaurant, not a bar: the ABC calls this the 'bona fide eating place' requirement.`,`Type 47 licenses generally cost more and take longer to acquire or transfer than Type 41.`,`Some Type 47 locations carry a Conditional Use Beverage (CUB) overlay from the city that adds its own restrictions on top of the state license.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`The short answer`,id:`the-short-answer`},{type:`p`,text:`A Type 41 license lets a restaurant serve beer and wine only. A Type 47 license lets a restaurant serve beer, wine, and full distilled spirits, a complete bar program. Both are 'on-sale' licenses meant for consumption on the premises, and both legally require the business to operate as a bona fide eating place, meaning food service is the primary function, not an afterthought to the bar.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Type 41: on-sale beer and wine, eating place`,id:`type-41-explained`},{type:`p`,text:`Type 41 is the right fit for concepts built around food where beer and wine round out the menu rather than drive the business: think casual dining, pizza, most fast-casual concepts, and neighborhood restaurants without a cocktail program. It's typically faster and less expensive to acquire than a Type 47, and in most parts of Los Angeles County it's easier to find available inventory for.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Covers: beer, wine, and sake for on-site consumption.`,`Does not cover: distilled spirits (vodka, whiskey, tequila, rum, gin, etc.) in any form, including in cocktails.`,`Requires: a genuine food-service operation, the ABC can and does verify this.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Type 47: on-sale general, eating place`,id:`type-47-explained`},{type:`p`,text:`Type 47 covers everything Type 41 does, plus full distilled spirits, meaning a complete cocktail program. It's the license most full-service restaurants with a bar area want, and it's generally the more expensive and harder-to-source of the two, particularly for a restaurant that wants an already-issued license rather than applying for a new one from the ABC directly.`},{type:`p`,text:`In some Los Angeles neighborhoods, a Type 47 location also carries a Conditional Use Beverage permit (a CUB) issued by the city or county on top of the state ABC license. A CUB can layer on its own conditions: hours of operation, occupancy limits, noise mitigation, or a requirement that alcohol sales stay under a certain percentage of total sales. Buyers need to review the CUB conditions attached to a specific address, not just assume the state license tells the whole story.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Type 41 vs. Type 47, side by side`,id:`comparison`},{type:`ul`,items:[`What's served: Type 41 is beer and wine. Type 47 is beer, wine, and spirits.`,`Typical concept fit: Type 41 is casual and fast-casual dining. Type 47 is full-service dining with a bar program.`,`Relative cost to acquire: Type 41 is generally lower-cost; Type 47 generally carries a higher premium in the open market.`,`Local overlay risk: Type 47 locations are more likely to carry a city or county CUB with additional operating conditions.`,`Eating-place requirement: applies to both, neither license permits operating primarily as a bar.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`How to actually get one in Los Angeles`,id:`how-to-get-one`},{type:`p`,text:`There are two paths: a person-to-person transfer of an existing license (buying one that's already issued, either standalone or as part of a restaurant acquisition), or a new application directly through the California Department of Alcoholic Beverage Control. Which path makes sense, and how long it takes, depends on current license availability for your license type in your specific area, and whether the address you're looking at already carries a CUB or other local entitlement. This is where a licensing specialist who works LA transactions regularly earns their fee: getting the wrong answer here can add months to a deal or kill it in escrow.`}],faqs:[{q:`What's the difference between a Type 41 and Type 47 liquor license?`,a:`Type 41 permits on-sale beer and wine only. Type 47 permits on-sale beer, wine, and full distilled spirits. Both require the business to operate primarily as a restaurant.`},{q:`Can I upgrade from a Type 41 to a Type 47 later?`,a:`You can't 'upgrade' a Type 41 into a Type 47. They're separate license types. You would need to acquire a Type 47 license (by transfer or new application) and hold it in place of, or alongside, your existing license, depending on your setup.`},{q:`Do I need a Type 47 to serve cocktails?`,a:`Yes. Any restaurant serving distilled spirits, including in mixed cocktails, needs a Type 47 (or another full-liquor license type). A Type 41 only covers beer and wine.`},{q:`What is a 'bona fide eating place' requirement?`,a:`It's the ABC's requirement that a business holding a Type 41 or Type 47 operates primarily as a restaurant, with a real kitchen, a real menu, and food as the core of the business, rather than functioning as a bar that happens to serve food.`},{q:`What is a CUB and does it affect my license?`,a:`A Conditional Use Beverage permit is a local land-use entitlement, separate from your state ABC license, that some cities and the county require for alcohol service at a given address. It can impose its own hours, occupancy, or operating conditions on top of what the state license allows, always check whether a specific location carries one before assuming the state license tells the whole story.`}],related:[{slug:`restaurant-valuation-los-angeles-2026`,title:`How Much Is My LA Restaurant Worth? A 2026 Valuation Guide`}]},{slug:`best-la-neighborhoods-open-restaurant-2026`,title:`Best LA Neighborhoods to Open a Restaurant in 2026`,seoTitle:`Best LA Neighborhoods to Open a Restaurant (2026) | Penthouse Advisors`,description:`A broker's-eye view of where to open a restaurant in Los Angeles in 2026, by foot traffic, price point, concept fit, and what to expect from landlords in each submarket.`,keywords:[`best neighborhoods open restaurant Los Angeles`,`where to open a restaurant in LA`,`LA restaurant real estate`,`restaurant space for lease Los Angeles neighborhoods`],category:`LA Market`,publishedAt:`2026-08-27`,readMinutes:9,keyTakeaways:[`LA isn't one restaurant market: rent, license availability, and landlord expectations vary block by block, not just neighborhood by neighborhood.`,`Match your concept to the neighborhood's actual foot traffic pattern (daytime office workers vs. evening/weekend destination diners) before you match it to the rent.`,`Second-generation restaurant spaces are available in every submarket below, ask for them specifically, since they save six figures in build-out.`,`A great concept in the wrong neighborhood underperforms a mediocre concept in the right one.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`How to actually pick a neighborhood`,id:`how-to-pick`},{type:`p`,text:`The mistake most first-time LA restaurant owners make is picking a neighborhood because they like eating there, then reverse-engineering a concept to fit. Work it the other direction: know your price point, your hours (lunch-driven vs. dinner-driven vs. all-day), and whether you need a liquor license before you tour a single space. The neighborhoods below are grouped by what actually drives their foot traffic, because that's what will drive your P&L.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Downtown LA / Arts District`,id:`downtown-arts-district`},{type:`p`,text:`A mix of dense residential loft conversions, office workers, and a growing evening/weekend destination-dining crowd. Concepts that do well here tend to lean into design and experience: this is a submarket where diners are willing to pay for atmosphere, not just food. Expect landlords accustomed to build-outs in converted industrial and pre-war commercial buildings, which can mean unusual ceiling heights, exposed infrastructure, and permitting quirks specific to older structures.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Silver Lake / Los Feliz / Echo Park`,id:`silver-lake-los-feliz`},{type:`p`,text:`Young, dense, walkable, and less tourist-dependent than the Westside, this is a resident-driven market where a strong local following matters more than street visibility. Coffee, wine bars, and mid-priced dinner concepts consistently perform well. Spaces here are often smaller-format, which keeps build-out costs down but puts pressure on menu efficiency and seat turnover.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Santa Monica / West LA / Sawtelle`,id:`santa-monica-west-la`},{type:`p`,text:`A blend of tourist traffic near the coast and a dense, affluent residential base further inland. Sawtelle in particular has become one of the most concentrated restaurant corridors in the city, which cuts both ways: high walk-by foot traffic, but real competition for attention on a single stretch. Rents and landlord expectations here run higher than most other submarkets on this list, and licensing (particularly full liquor) tends to be more competitively sought after.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Culver City`,id:`culver-city`},{type:`p`,text:`Culver City has spent the last several years building a genuine restaurant identity of its own, driven by nearby office and studio employment plus a walkable downtown core. It sits at a more moderate price point than Santa Monica while still drawing a dining-out crowd with real spending power, a common sweet spot for operators priced out of the Westside's most expensive corridors.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Pasadena / San Gabriel Valley`,id:`pasadena-sgv`},{type:`p`,text:`A different rhythm than the Westside: more family-oriented, more weekday-lunch-driven around office and civic employment, and historically home to some of the region's strongest independent and multi-generational restaurant concepts. Rents are generally more accessible than west-of-downtown submarkets, and the area rewards operators who build genuine neighborhood loyalty over one that chases trend-driven traffic.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Beverly Hills / West Hollywood`,id:`beverly-hills-weho`},{type:`p`,text:`The highest price point on this list, in both rent and check average, with a customer base that expects a polished, full-service experience. This is where a Type 47 license and a serious wine or cocktail program pay for themselves fastest, but it's also where landlord expectations, buildout standards, and lease terms are the most demanding. Not the right submarket for a first-time operator testing a concept on a tight budget.`},{type:`h2`,text:`One shortcut that applies everywhere on this list`,id:`second-gen-shortcut`},{type:`callout`,title:`Ask about second-generation spaces first`,text:`In every neighborhood above, a second-generation restaurant space, one with an existing hood, grease trap, and kitchen infrastructure already in place, cuts build-out cost dramatically compared to a raw shell. Before falling in love with a raw retail space in your target neighborhood, ask your broker what second-gen inventory is available there right now.`}],faqs:[{q:`What's the best LA neighborhood for a first-time restaurant owner?`,a:`Neighborhoods with more moderate rents and a loyal resident dining base, like Silver Lake, Culver City, or parts of the San Gabriel Valley, generally give first-time operators more room to find their footing than the highest-rent Westside corridors.`},{q:`Which LA neighborhoods have the highest restaurant rents?`,a:`Beverly Hills, West Hollywood, and the Santa Monica / Sawtelle corridor consistently carry the highest restaurant rents in the county, driven by affluent customer bases and concentrated foot traffic.`},{q:`Is it better to open in a tourist area or a residential neighborhood?`,a:`It depends on your concept and hours. Tourist-driven areas support higher check averages and destination dining but can go quiet in slow seasons. Resident-driven neighborhoods offer steadier, more predictable traffic but reward genuine local loyalty over novelty.`},{q:`Do liquor license costs vary by LA neighborhood?`,a:`Effective cost and availability can vary by area, particularly for Type 47 licenses in neighborhoods with additional local Conditional Use Beverage requirements. Always check license availability and any local overlay conditions for a specific address before committing to a location.`},{q:`What is a second-generation restaurant space?`,a:`A space previously operated as a restaurant, with existing kitchen infrastructure (hood, grease trap, plumbing) still in place. It typically saves a new operator significant build-out cost compared to converting a raw retail shell.`}],related:[{slug:`second-generation-restaurant-space-explained`,title:`What Is a Second-Generation Restaurant Space?`}]},{slug:`restaurant-buyer-due-diligence-checklist`,title:`The Restaurant Buyer's Due Diligence Checklist: What to Check Before You Sign`,seoTitle:`Restaurant Buyer Due Diligence Checklist | Penthouse Advisors`,description:`What to check before your deposit becomes non-refundable: financials, lease, licenses, equipment, and health department history. A buyer's due diligence checklist for LA restaurant deals.`,keywords:[`restaurant due diligence checklist`,`buying a restaurant checklist`,`restaurant business investigation`,`what to check before buying a restaurant`],category:`Buying`,publishedAt:`2026-08-27`,readMinutes:8,keyTakeaways:[`Due diligence exists to protect your deposit: most of it should happen before your deposit becomes non-refundable.`,`Financial records, the lease, and license status cause more failed deals than food quality or concept ever will.`,`Always verify equipment ownership separately: leased equipment represented as owned is one of the most common surprises.`,`Pull the Health Department's inspection history yourself; don't rely on the seller's summary of it.`,`A business investigation isn't about finding a reason to walk away, it's about knowing exactly what you're buying.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`The short answer`,id:`the-short-answer`},{type:`p`,text:`Before you sign anything binding, verify five things: the financials tie out, the lease can actually be assigned to you on acceptable terms, the liquor license (if any) is transferable and clean, the equipment you're buying is actually owned by the seller, and the Health Department file has no open violations you're inheriting. Everything else in due diligence supports one of those five checks.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Financial records`,id:`financial-records`},{type:`ul`,items:[`24 months of POS reports, reconciled against bank deposits, not just summarized totals.`,`Sales tax filings for the same period, cross-checked against reported revenue.`,`Payroll records and current staffing costs, including any owner or family compensation you'll need to replace.`,`Vendor and supplier statements, to confirm the cost of goods sold is representative of a normal month.`,`Any outstanding loans, equipment leases, or liens against the business.`]},{type:`p`,text:`If a seller can't produce clean, reconciled numbers for this period, treat that as information, not an inconvenience: it usually means either the books were never kept tightly, or there's cash activity that never made it onto the books at all. Either way, it changes how much you should trust the stated SDE.`},{type:`h2`,text:`The lease`,id:`the-lease`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Term remaining and any renewal options: read the actual renewal language, not just the summary.`,`Assignment clause: what does the landlord require to approve you as the new tenant?`,`Current rent versus market rent for the area, and any scheduled increases.`,`Landlord estoppel certificate, confirming the lease terms as the seller has represented them.`,`Any personal guaranty requirements the landlord will impose on you as the incoming tenant.`]},{type:`p`,text:`Get an estoppel from the landlord before you're deep into escrow. It's the document that confirms, in writing, that the lease terms you've been told match what the landlord will actually honor, and it surfaces landlord-side problems (unpaid CAM charges, disputed repairs) before they become your problem.`},{type:`h2`,text:`License and permit status`,id:`license-and-permit-status`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Current ABC license status: active, in good standing, and free of pending accusations or discipline.`,`Whether the license transfer requires ABC approval of you personally (it does), and how long that typically takes for your license type.`,`Health Department permit status and whether a change-of-ownership permit will be required.`,`Any local Conditional Use Beverage (CUB) conditions attached to the address, separate from the state license.`,`Building and fire department sign-offs, particularly occupancy limits and any unpermitted construction.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Equipment ownership`,id:`equipment-ownership`},{type:`p`,text:`This is one of the most common, and most avoidable, surprises in a restaurant sale. Walk the kitchen with an itemized equipment list and confirm, piece by piece, what's owned outright versus what's financed or leased. A seller representing leased equipment as an asset you're buying, whether intentionally or by mistake, leaves you responsible for a lease payment on equipment you thought you owned free and clear.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Health Department history`,id:`health-department-history`},{type:`p`,text:`Pull the inspection history directly from LA County Public Health rather than relying on the seller's account of it. Look specifically for repeat violations (a pattern matters more than a single incident), any red-tag closures, and whether prior corrective actions were actually closed out or just noted and left open.`},{type:`callout`,title:`One more thing worth checking`,text:`Ask specifically whether the space is a second-generation restaurant build-out, and if so, confirm the hood, grease trap, and grease interceptor still pass current inspection standards. An older second-gen space can still require real money to bring current.`}],faqs:[{q:`What should I check before buying a restaurant?`,a:`At minimum: 24 months of reconciled financials, the lease and its assignment terms, liquor license and permit status, equipment ownership, and the Health Department's full inspection history for the location.`},{q:`How long does restaurant due diligence usually take?`,a:`Most thorough due diligence runs 2–4 weeks once you have full access to records, though license and permit verification through the ABC and Health Department can extend that timeline depending on current processing times.`},{q:`What is a business investigation in a restaurant sale?`,a:`A business investigation is a structured pre-purchase review: pulling public records, verifying licenses and permits, and cross-checking financials, done before your deposit becomes non-refundable, so you know exactly what you're acquiring.`},{q:`Can I back out of a restaurant purchase after finding a problem in due diligence?`,a:`That depends entirely on how your purchase agreement and deposit terms are written. This is exactly why due diligence contingencies and deposit refundability need to be negotiated clearly in the LOI before you sign, not figured out after you find a problem.`},{q:`Who should I bring in to help with due diligence?`,a:`A restaurant-specific broker or advisor to coordinate the process, a CPA to review the financials, and an attorney to review the lease assignment and purchase agreement. General small-business templates often miss restaurant-specific issues like license transfer timing and health permit continuity.`}],related:[{slug:`second-generation-restaurant-space-explained`,title:`What Is a Second-Generation Restaurant Space?`},{slug:`restaurant-valuation-los-angeles-2026`,title:`How Much Is My LA Restaurant Worth? A 2026 Valuation Guide`}]},{slug:`second-generation-restaurant-space-explained`,title:`What Is a Second-Generation Restaurant Space? (And Why It Can Save You Six Figures)`,seoTitle:`Second-Generation Restaurant Space Explained | Penthouse Advisors`,description:`A second-generation restaurant space already has a hood, grease trap, and kitchen infrastructure in place. Here's what it means, what to still verify, and where to find one in LA.`,keywords:[`second generation restaurant space`,`second gen restaurant space Los Angeles`,`restaurant space with existing hood`,`turnkey restaurant space LA`],category:`Buying`,publishedAt:`2026-08-27`,readMinutes:6,keyTakeaways:[`A second-generation space previously operated as a restaurant and still has kitchen infrastructure in place.`,`Existing hood, grease trap, and grease interceptor systems are the single biggest build-out cost a second-gen space saves you.`,`'Second-gen' doesn't mean 'move-in ready': permits, equipment condition, and code compliance still need to be verified.`,`These spaces turn over constantly in LA as restaurants close or relocate: ask your broker for current inventory before touring raw shells.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`The short answer`,id:`the-short-answer`},{type:`p`,text:`A second-generation restaurant space is a location that previously operated as a restaurant and still has some or all of the kitchen infrastructure in place, most importantly the exhaust hood, grease trap or interceptor, and often walk-in refrigeration and existing plumbing runs. Building that infrastructure from scratch in a raw retail shell (a 'first-generation' or 'vanilla box' space) is one of the largest line items in any restaurant build-out, which is why second-gen spaces are so valuable to buyers on a budget or a timeline.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Why it matters for your build-out cost`,id:`why-it-matters`},{type:`p`,text:`A commercial kitchen hood system with fire suppression, ductwork, and make-up air, plus a grease interceptor tied into the sewer line, routinely represents one of the largest single categories of a ground-up restaurant build-out, and that's before walk-in coolers, plumbing for a full kitchen, and the electrical service upgrade a commercial kitchen typically requires. A second-gen space that already has usable versions of this infrastructure can turn a project that would otherwise take months of permitting and construction into one focused mainly on cosmetic build-out and equipment.`},{type:`h2`,text:`What 'second-gen' does not mean`,id:`what-it-doesnt-mean`},{type:`p`,text:`Second-generation is not a guarantee of move-in-ready condition. It means the infrastructure exists, not that it currently passes inspection, matches your concept's equipment layout, or is sized correctly for your intended volume. Treat every second-gen space as a starting point to verify, not a finished product to assume.`},{type:`h3`,text:`What to verify even in a second-gen space`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Does the hood and fire suppression system currently pass inspection, or has it lapsed since the last tenant?`,`Is the grease trap or interceptor sized appropriately for your concept, and has it been maintained?`,`Are the electrical service and gas line capacity sufficient for your equipment plan, not just the prior tenant's?`,`What permits does the space carry, and do they transfer or require a new plan-check submittal for a change of use or ownership?`,`Is there any unpermitted work from a prior tenant that the city will flag once you file for your own permits?`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Where to find one in LA`,id:`where-to-find-one`},{type:`p`,text:`Second-gen inventory turns over constantly as restaurants close, relocate, or get acquired, but it rarely gets marketed as a distinct category on general commercial listing sites. A broker working LA restaurant deals specifically will usually know what's available off-market or about to hit the market before it's broadly listed, which matters because these spaces tend to move fast once they're identified.`},{type:`callout`,title:`Buying the business vs. taking over the space`,text:`Sometimes a second-gen space comes attached to an operating business for sale, and sometimes it's simply a vacant space with a prior restaurant's infrastructure left behind. The due diligence is different for each: an operating business sale requires the full financial and license review; a vacant second-gen space is more about verifying the physical infrastructure and permit status.`}],faqs:[{q:`What does 'second-generation restaurant space' mean?`,a:`It refers to a commercial space that previously operated as a restaurant and retains some or all of the kitchen infrastructure (hood, grease trap, plumbing), reducing build-out cost and time for the next tenant.`},{q:`Is a second-generation space always cheaper to build out?`,a:`It's usually significantly cheaper than a raw shell, but not automatically move-in ready. Equipment condition, code compliance, and whether the layout fits your concept all still need to be verified and can still require real investment.`},{q:`Do I need new permits if I take over a second-gen restaurant space?`,a:`In most cases yes: a change of ownership or change of use typically triggers a new health permit review and often a plan-check submittal, even when the physical infrastructure stays largely the same.`},{q:`How do I find second-generation restaurant spaces in Los Angeles?`,a:`General commercial real estate listings rarely categorize spaces this way. A broker who works restaurant transactions specifically typically has visibility into current and upcoming second-gen inventory, including off-market opportunities.`},{q:`Can I change the concept in a second-gen space, or does it have to match the prior restaurant?`,a:`You can generally change concept, but the existing infrastructure was built for the prior tenant's needs: a heavy-grease concept's hood and grease interceptor may be oversized or undersized for a very different type of kitchen, so verify fit rather than assuming it.`}],related:[{slug:`restaurant-buyer-due-diligence-checklist`,title:`The Restaurant Buyer's Due Diligence Checklist`},{slug:`best-la-neighborhoods-open-restaurant-2026`,title:`Best LA Neighborhoods to Open a Restaurant in 2026`}]}].sort((e,t)=>e.publishedAt<t.publishedAt?1:-1);function Q(e){return bd.find(t=>t.slug===e)}function xd(){return bd}var Sd=D(`/blog/$slug`)({loader:({params:e})=>{let t=Q(e.slug);if(!t)throw xe();return t},head:({loaderData:e,params:t})=>{let n=`/blog/${t.slug}`;if(!e)return{meta:[{title:`Post not found | Penthouse Advisors Blog`},{name:`robots`,content:`noindex`}]};let r=e;return{meta:[{title:r.seoTitle},{name:`description`,content:r.description},{name:`keywords`,content:r.keywords.join(`, `)},{property:`og:title`,content:r.seoTitle},{property:`og:description`,content:r.description},{property:`og:url`,content:`${Fe}${n}`},{property:`og:type`,content:`article`},{name:`twitter:card`,content:`summary_large_image`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:`${Fe}${n}`}],scripts:[Ne([{name:`Home`,path:`/`},{name:`Blog`,path:`/blog`},{name:r.title,path:n}]),De({headline:r.title,description:r.description,path:n,datePublished:r.publishedAt,dateModified:r.updatedAt??r.publishedAt}),...r.faqs.length?[ke(r.faqs)]:[]]}},component:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./blog._slug-D94n0L42.js`),__vite__mapDeps([35,2,1,3,4,36,37])),`component`),notFoundComponent:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./blog._slug-DTDUHu6b.js`),__vite__mapDeps([38,1,2,3,4])),`notFoundComponent`)}),Cd=D(`/restaurants-for-sale/`)({head:()=>({meta:[{title:`Restaurants For Sale in Los Angeles - Penthouse Advisors`},{name:`description`,content:`Current LA restaurants for sale and restaurant asset sales. Turnkey concepts, hoods, lease assignments. Updated weekly.`},{property:`og:title`,content:`LA Restaurants For Sale - Penthouse Advisors`},{property:`og:description`,content:`Turnkey restaurants and asset sales across Los Angeles County.`},{property:`og:url`,content:`https://www.penthouseadvisors.com/restaurants-for-sale`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:`https://www.penthouseadvisors.com/restaurants-for-sale`}],scripts:[{type:`application/ld+json`,children:JSON.stringify({"@context":`https://schema.org`,"@type":`BreadcrumbList`,itemListElement:[{"@type":`ListItem`,position:1,name:`Home`,item:`https://www.penthouseadvisors.com/`},{"@type":`ListItem`,position:2,name:`Restaurants For Sale`,item:`https://www.penthouseadvisors.com/restaurants-for-sale`}]})}]}),component:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./restaurants-for-sale.index-CAFqWYZA.js`),__vite__mapDeps([39,2,1,3,4,40])),`component`)}),wd=[{id:`R-004`,slug:`westwood-santa-monica-restaurant-asset-sale`,type:`Asset Sale`,title:`Restaurant Fully Equipped, Open, Permitted Asset Sale`,location:`NW corner of Westwood Blvd & Santa Monica Blvd, Los Angeles`,coords:[34.043,-118.4436],price:`$125,000`,priceNote:`Cash`,leaseType:`Asset sale · Fully equipped, open & permitted restaurant`,badge:`Available Now`,description:`Restaurant Fully Equipped, Open, Permitted Asset Sale Located in a vibrant commercial corridor on the northwest corner of Westwood and Santa Monica Boulevards. Charming building with restaurant on the ground floor. Off-street parking available for customers, and plenty of metered spots as well. Close to major public transportation routes with unobstructed exposure to the intersections of Santa Monica and Westwood (100,000 cars/day traffic count).`,features:[`Fully equipped, open and permitted`,`Vibrant commercial corridor`,`Charming building with restaurant on the ground floor`,`Off-street parking for customers`,`Plenty of metered street parking`,`Close to major public transportation routes`,`Unobstructed exposure at Santa Monica & Westwood`,`100,000 cars/day traffic count`],images:[`/assets/westwood-santa-monica-restaurant-1-f5ygtO_N.jpg`]},{id:`RE-005`,slug:`dental-office-for-lease`,type:`Medical / Dental`,title:`Therapy & Dental Space For Lease`,location:`West LA`,price:`$4,800 – $5,850 / month`,priceNote:`All-in · First & last month and security deposit`,leaseType:`Doctor, therapy & dental offices · Building zoned medical use`,badge:`Available Now`,description:`Doctor, therapy and dental offices in a building zoned medical use, West LA. Available inside a beautiful building. The second floor offices for lease are a 7 room doctor/therapy office at $5,850 per month all-in, plus a 4 room, plumbed dental office at $4,800 per month all-in. Available now.`,longDescription:[`Doctor, Therapy & Dental Offices West LA — offices in a building zoned medical use, West LA, available inside a beautiful building.`,`The second floor offices for lease are a 7 room doctor/therapy office at $5,850 per month all-in, plus a 4 room, plumbed dental office at $4,800 per month all-in. Available now.`,`First & last month and security deposit.`],sections:[{heading:`Second Floor — Doctor/Therapy Office`,body:`Doctor/therapy office with 7 rooms. Rent $5,850 per month, all-in. Available now.`,bullets:[`7 rooms`,`$5,850 per month, all-in`,`First & last month and security deposit`]},{heading:`Second Floor — Plumbed Dental Office`,body:`4 room, plumbed dental office. Rent $4,800 per month, all-in. Available now.`,bullets:[`4 rooms, plumbed`,`$4,800 per month, all-in`,`First & last month and security deposit`]}],features:[`Building zoned medical use`,`Doctor, therapy & dental offices`,`Beautiful building, West LA`,`Second floor 7 room doctor/therapy office`,`$5,850/month, all-in`,`4 room, plumbed dental office`,`$4,800/month, all-in`,`Available now`,`First & last month and security deposit`],images:[`/assets/dental-office-for-lease-D929-QbN.png`]},{id:`R-002`,slug:`santa-monica-asset-sale`,type:`Asset Sale`,title:`Santa Monica Restaurant — Turnkey Asset Sale`,location:`Santa Monica (Los Angeles County)`,coords:[34.0195,-118.4912],price:`$80,000`,priceNote:`Available — Open & Active`,sqft:`≈ 950 sq ft`,leaseType:`Assignment of lease · Shopping center`,badge:`Available Now`,description:`Health Department permitted, turnkey restaurant in a Santa Monica shopping center. Open and operating with a fully equipped kitchen, 18-foot hood, and extra-large walk-in refrigerator.`,longDescription:[`Turn-key, open-to-the-public, fully operational restaurant in a Santa Monica shopping center. Health Department permitted with an assignment of lease available to a qualified buyer.`,`The 950 sq ft space is fitted out with a full commercial cooking line under an 18-foot hood and an extra-large walk-in refrigerator — ready for day-one service with no buildout delay.`],sections:[{heading:`Deal Structure`,body:`Asset sale with assignment of lease. Allocation supports the $80,000 purchase price.`,bullets:[`Furniture, Fixtures & Equipment value: $35,000`,`Buildout value: $60,000`,`Goodwill: $50,000`,`Health Department permitted`,`Open and active operation`]},{heading:`Lease Terms`,body:`Assignment of lease in an established Santa Monica shopping center with steady foot traffic.`,bullets:[`Base rent: $4,369.70 (first month)`,`CAM: $1,771.50`,`Total rent: $6,141.20`,`3% annual increases`,`Shopping center location`]},{heading:`Included Equipment`,body:`Complete commercial cooking line and cold storage in place — turnkey for the next operator.`,bullets:[`18-foot Type 1 hood`,`In-ground grease interceptor`,`Extra-large walk-in refrigerator`,`Convection oven`,`Vertical broiler`,`6-burner stove`,`Flat grill`,`Deep fryer`,`Multi shelving and assorted racks`]}],features:[`≈ 950 sq ft turnkey restaurant`,`Health Department permitted`,`18-foot hood with full cooking line`,`Extra-large walk-in refrigerator`,`Open and active`,`Shopping center location`],images:[`/assets/santa-monica-1-BuxnW7n7.png`,`/assets/santa-monica-2-Kz3I5-aZ.png`]},{id:`R-003`,slug:`wilshire-second-gen-lease`,type:`Asset Sale`,title:`Gym and Coffee Shop Facility on the entire Property Lot`,location:`2408 Wilshire Blvd, Santa Monica, CA`,coords:[34.032541,-118.484189],price:`$10,000 / month All-In`,priceNote:`Rent starts at $8,825 plus NNN · Long lease available`,sqft:`1,150 sq ft`,leaseType:`Single story, stand-alone building · Use: Gym & Coffee Shop`,badge:`Available Now`,description:`Gym and Coffee Shop Facility on the entire Property Lot. The Lot has a 1,150 sqft., Single Story, Stand-alone air conditioned building for Gym and Coffee Shop, equipped with a commercial kitchen to prepare high quality coffee and Smoothies, plus a walk-in refrigerator.`,longDescription:[`Gym and Coffee Shop Facility on the entire Property Lot.`,`The Lot has a 1,150 sqft., Single Story, Stand-alone air conditioned building for Gym and Coffee Shop, equipped with a commercial kitchen to prepare high quality coffee and Smoothies, plus a walk-in refrigerator.`,`Key: The Property has a large, covered rear area gated for outdoor Gym. Private Employee Rear Parking.`,`Long lease is available. Rent starts at $8,825 plus NNN, TOTAL $10,000 per month all-in.`,`Call to Visit this Santa Monica opportunity at 2408 Wilshire Blvd., and 24th Street.`],sections:[{heading:`Lease Opportunity`,body:`Gym and Coffee Shop Facility on the entire Property Lot, with a large, covered, gated rear area for an outdoor Gym and private employee rear parking.`,bullets:[`1,150 sqft., single story, stand-alone building`,`Air conditioned`,`Commercial kitchen for high quality coffee and smoothies`,`Walk-in refrigerator`,`Large, covered rear area gated for outdoor Gym`,`Private employee rear parking`,`Long lease is available`,`Rent starts at $8,825 plus NNN`,`TOTAL $10,000 per month all-in`]}],features:[`1,150 sqft. stand-alone building`,`Single story, air conditioned`,`Use: Gym & Coffee Shop`,`Commercial kitchen & walk-in refrigerator`,`Covered, gated rear area for outdoor Gym`,`Private employee rear parking`,`Long lease available`,`$8,825 plus NNN · $10,000 per month all-in`,`Wilshire at 24th Street, Santa Monica`],images:[`/assets/wilshire-3-BMdDSqQH.png`,`/assets/wilshire-1-CwhkyxGJ.png`,`/assets/wilshire-2-Da7hldQq.png`,`/assets/wilshire-4-8CLY3-Jf.png`]},{id:`B-001`,slug:`hardware-store-for-sale`,type:`Business Sale`,title:`Hardware Store for Sale`,location:`Westwood Blvd, West LA`,price:`$175,000`,priceNote:`Cash`,leaseType:`Business sale · Retail`,badge:`Available Now`,description:`Established hardware store for sale on Westwood Blvd, West LA. $175,000 cash. Annual revenue $402,000. Established 2020.`,longDescription:[`Established hardware store for sale on Westwood Blvd in West Los Angeles.`,`Asking $175,000 cash. Annual revenue of approximately $402,000. Business established in 2020 with one full-time employee.`,`New lease to be negotiated with three 3-year extension options. Product categories include key duplication, tools, gardening, plumbing, electrical, home hardware, and more.`],sections:[{heading:`Deal Highlights`,body:`Straightforward business sale with training and escrow support included.`,bullets:[`Asking price: $175,000 cash`,`Annual revenue: approximately $402,000`,`Established: 2020`,`Employees: 1 full-time`,`4 weeks of support & training included`,`Co-brokering welcome`]},{heading:`Lease Terms`,body:`Lease commenced October 9, 2020. New lease terms to be negotiated with the landlord.`,bullets:[`Base rent at commencement: $3,082.56 / mo`,`Year 6 monthly base rent: $4,840.89 + CAM`,`Three (3) 3-year options to extend`,`Buyer reimburses Seller's security deposit through escrow`,`FF&E sold in as-is condition`]}],features:[`Established hardware store`,`Westwood Blvd, West LA location`,`$175,000 cash`,`$402,000 annual revenue`,`Key duplication, tools, plumbing, electrical`,`New lease negotiable`],images:[Be,Ve]}];function Td(e){return wd.find(t=>t.slug===e)}var Ed=D(`/restaurants-for-sale/$slug`)({loader:({params:e})=>{let t=Td(e.slug);if(!t)throw xe();return{listing:t}},head:({params:e,loaderData:t})=>{let n=t?.listing,r=`https://www.penthouseadvisors.com/restaurants-for-sale/${e.slug}`;if(!n)return{meta:[{title:`Listing — Penthouse Advisors`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:r}]};let i=n.description??n.longDescription?.[0]??n.title,a=n.images?.[0];return{meta:[{title:`${n.title} — Penthouse Advisors`},{name:`description`,content:i},{property:`og:title`,content:n.title},{property:`og:description`,content:i},{property:`og:url`,content:r},{property:`og:type`,content:`article`},...a?[{property:`og:image`,content:Ee(a)},{name:`twitter:image`,content:Ee(a)}]:[]],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:r}]}},notFoundComponent:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./restaurants-for-sale._slug-CLx8mqVG.js`),__vite__mapDeps([41,3,2,4])),`notFoundComponent`),errorComponent:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./restaurants-for-sale._slug-CmCJ9Os4.js`),__vite__mapDeps([42,4,2])),`errorComponent`),component:w(()=>E(()=>import(`./restaurants-for-sale._slug-DcM9qY92.js`),__vite__mapDeps([43,2,1,3,4,9,10,6])),`component`)}),Dd={slug:`how-to-buy-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles`,seoTitle:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles (2026 Guide)`,description:`Step-by-step guide to buying a restaurant in Los Angeles County: due diligence, financing, escrow, ABC and Health permits, lease assignment, and closing.`,keywords:[`how to buy a restaurant in Los Angeles`,`buying a restaurant in California`,`restaurant acquisition Los Angeles`,`buy a restaurant LA`,`restaurant broker Los Angeles`],updatedAt:`2026-07-24`,readMinutes:11,keyTakeaways:[`Most LA restaurant acquisitions close in 60–120 days once an offer is accepted.`,`The lease is usually the single biggest risk — assignment consent and remaining term make or break the deal.`,`ABC liquor licenses transfer through escrow; expect a 60–90 day ABC review.`,`Los Angeles County Health Dept requires a permit change of ownership and often a plan check for altered layouts.`,`Verify seller-declared sales against POS, bank deposits, and sales-tax filings — never off the P&L alone.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`Decide what you're actually buying`},{type:`p`,text:`Restaurant deals in Los Angeles come in three shapes: an asset sale (equipment, goodwill, and the lease assignment), a business/stock sale (the operating entity with its liabilities), or a real-estate purchase where you buy the building along with the operation. The tax, licensing, and liability profile of each is very different — decide with your CPA before you write an offer.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Set your budget and financing`},{type:`p`,text:`Independent LA restaurants change hands between roughly $150,000 for a small turnkey space and $2M+ for a full liquor concept in a prime submarket. Plan for the purchase price, working capital for 90 days, a security deposit and first month's rent for the landlord, ABC transfer costs, Health Dept plan-check fees, and a reserve for surprises found during inspection.`},{type:`h3`,text:`Common financing paths`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Cash — fastest close, strongest position.`,`SBA 7(a) loan — up to $5M, typical 10-year term, requires strong seller books.`,`Seller carry — the seller finances 20–40% at negotiated terms.`,`E-2 visa investment — for European entrepreneurs using the restaurant as their qualifying investment.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Find qualified listings`},{type:`p`,text:`Public listings surface a fraction of LA's real inventory. Most serious deals move off-market through brokers who know which operators are quietly tired, which landlords want a new tenant, and which spaces are about to be listed. That's why buyers work with a specialized restaurant broker rather than a generalist commercial broker.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Write the LOI and open escrow`},{type:`p`,text:`A Letter of Intent locks in price, deposit, contingencies, and a due-diligence window (typically 21–45 days). Once accepted, escrow opens at a licensed California bulk-sale escrow house — a Bulk Sale Notice is published to protect you from the seller's creditors, which alone takes 12–15 business days.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Run real due diligence`},{type:`p`,text:`This is where deals live or die. A thorough pre-purchase investigation covers:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Lease terms: remaining years, options, rent escalators, personal guarantees, assignment consent language, use clause.`,`Financials: last 24 months of POS reports, bank deposits, sales-tax returns (BOE-401), and credit-card processor statements — reconciled against the seller's P&L.`,`ABC license status: type, conditions (CUB), disciplinary history, and whether it will transfer with the sale.`,`Health Dept history: current grade card, red-tag history, open violations, and whether your planned menu triggers a new plan check.`,`Building & Safety: unpermitted construction, expired permits, ADA exposure.`,`Equipment: age, condition, whether items are owned or leased.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Handle the lease assignment`},{type:`p`,text:`Every LA restaurant lease requires landlord consent to assign. Landlords use this leverage to raise rent, extend the term, tighten the guaranty, or refuse entirely. Start the assignment conversation with the landlord within the first week of escrow — a stalled assignment is the number-one reason LA restaurant deals collapse.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Transfer the ABC license`},{type:`p`,text:`If the restaurant sells beer, wine, or spirits, you'll file a person-to-person transfer with the California Department of Alcoholic Beverage Control. Expect 60–90 days for ABC to post, investigate, and issue. During that window, most buyers operate under a temporary retail permit for continuity.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Change the Health permit`},{type:`p`,text:`Los Angeles County Environmental Health requires a Change of Ownership application and inspection before you can operate under your name. If you alter the layout, add cooking equipment, or change the menu category, you may also trigger a full plan check — that adds 4–12 weeks and requires stamped blueprints.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Close and take possession`},{type:`p`,text:`At closing, escrow disburses funds, records the bill of sale, and hands you keys. On day one you should have a working POS, a payroll provider, a food distributor account, a permit board posted, and your ABC temporary in the register drawer.`}],faqs:[{q:`How long does it take to buy a restaurant in Los Angeles?`,a:`From accepted offer to keys, plan on 60–120 days. The gating items are landlord consent on the lease assignment, the ABC person-to-person transfer, and the LA County Health Dept change of ownership. Cash deals with no license transfer and a cooperative landlord can close in 45 days.`},{q:`How much money do I need to buy a restaurant in LA?`,a:`Budget the purchase price plus 20–30% for working capital, deposits, permit fees, and reserves. A $400,000 restaurant purchase typically needs $475,000–$525,000 in total capital.`},{q:`Do I need experience to buy a restaurant?`,a:`No, but landlords, lenders, and the ABC will weight your application heavily on hospitality experience. First-time buyers usually strengthen their file with an experienced operating partner or a general manager already on the ground.`},{q:`Can I keep the current employees?`,a:`Yes. Most LA restaurant asset sales retain the staff on day one — the new owner rehires them under the new EIN. You'll need workers' comp, payroll, and I-9 verification set up before close.`},{q:`What happens if the landlord won't approve the assignment?`,a:`The deal doesn't close unless your LOI made landlord consent a contingency (it should have). We negotiate directly with landlords daily and know which levers to pull — rent bumps, extended terms, or additional guaranty language usually break the logjam.`}],related:[{category:`buying-a-restaurant`,slug:`restaurant-due-diligence-checklist`,title:`Restaurant Due Diligence Checklist`},{category:`alcohol-licensing`,slug:`abc-license-transfer-guide`,title:`ABC License Transfer Guide`},{category:`lease-assignments`,slug:`restaurant-lease-assignment-guide`,title:`Restaurant Lease Assignment Guide`}]},Od={slug:`restaurant-due-diligence-checklist`,title:`Restaurant Due Diligence Checklist`,seoTitle:`Restaurant Due Diligence Checklist for LA Buyers`,description:`The complete pre-purchase due diligence checklist for Los Angeles restaurant buyers: lease, financials, ABC license, Health Dept history, equipment, and permits.`,keywords:[`restaurant due diligence checklist`,`restaurant due diligence Los Angeles`,`buying an existing restaurant due diligence`,`restaurant acquisition checklist`],updatedAt:`2026-07-24`,readMinutes:8,keyTakeaways:[`Never accept a seller's P&L at face value — reconcile against POS, bank, and sales-tax filings.`,`The lease review is the single most valuable line item in a restaurant due diligence.`,`Pull the ABC license file directly from the state — sellers routinely omit CUB restrictions.`,`Health Dept red-tag history and open plan-check items follow the location, not the operator.`,`Get every leased equipment contract in writing before you assume you own the hood.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`Why due diligence matters`},{type:`p`,text:`A restaurant looks the same from the sidewalk whether it's clean or riddled with liabilities. Due diligence is the process of getting behind the sidewalk view — verifying the lease, the license, the permits, the numbers, and the equipment — before your deposit becomes non-refundable.`},{type:`h2`,text:`1. Lease review`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Full lease with every amendment and estoppel.`,`Remaining term and every renewal option (in writing, not verbal).`,`Base rent, CAM, insurance, taxes, and how they escalate.`,`Assignment and change-of-control clauses — who has to approve, and what standards can they apply?`,`Personal guaranty language — is it good-guy, full recourse, or burn-off?`,`Use clause — does it permit your intended concept or restrict alcohol/hours?`,`Exclusives, radius restrictions, co-tenancy, kick-out rights.`,`Landlord's current standing with the property (foreclosure, tax liens).`]},{type:`h2`,text:`2. Financial verification`},{type:`ul`,items:[`24 months of monthly POS sales reports (Toast, Square, Aloha exports).`,`24 months of bank statements — reconcile deposits against POS.`,`24 months of merchant processor statements.`,`Trailing four sales-tax returns (California CDTFA / BOE-401).`,`Federal tax returns for the operating entity (last two).`,`P&L, balance sheet, and A/R aging.`,`Payroll register — verify tip reporting and workers' comp classifications.`,`Vendor invoices for the last three months (food cost verification).`]},{type:`h2`,text:`3. ABC license status`},{type:`ul`,items:[`License type (41, 47, 48, etc.) and premises address.`,`Every Conditional Use Beverage (CUB) restriction — hours, entertainment, food-service ratios.`,`Disciplinary history from the ABC file.`,`Any pending accusations or license holds.`,`Confirmation the license is transferable person-to-person.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`4. Health Department history`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Current grade card and last 24 months of inspection reports.`,`Red-tag history and any current open violations.`,`Whether the current permit covers your planned menu and equipment.`,`Open plan-check items or required corrections tied to the address.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`5. Building & Safety`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Certificate of Occupancy — confirms legal use of the premises.`,`Permit history — any unpermitted construction becomes your liability.`,`ADA exposure — restrooms, path of travel, parking.`,`Fire Dept clearance and current extinguisher/hood-suppression tags.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`6. Equipment and personal property`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Itemized equipment list with age and condition.`,`Which items are owned outright vs. leased (POS, ice machine, dish machine, soda system).`,`Copies of every equipment lease with payoff or transfer terms.`,`Serial numbers on hood, walk-in, ovens — confirm they match the bill of sale.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`7. Contracts and staff`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Every service contract: pest, grease, hood cleaning, linen, waste, POS, music.`,`Employee census with wage, position, tenure, and I-9 status.`,`Any union or collective bargaining coverage.`,`Open workers' comp claims or wage-and-hour actions.`]}],faqs:[{q:`How long should due diligence take on a restaurant purchase?`,a:`Standard LA due-diligence windows are 21–45 days. Complex deals with a full plan check, ABC investigation, or unusual lease terms often need 60.`},{q:`What is the biggest hidden risk in a restaurant purchase?`,a:`Unpermitted construction. If the previous owner added a bar, extended the hood, or converted storage into seating without permits, the liability transfers to you the moment you close.`},{q:`Can I do due diligence myself?`,a:`You can pull most public records yourself, but the interpretation — what a CUB condition actually forbids, whether a lease's assignment clause is workable, whether the sales numbers reconcile — is where experienced advisors save buyers six figures.`}],related:[{category:`buying-a-restaurant`,slug:`how-to-buy-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles`},{category:`business-investigations`,slug:`what-is-a-restaurant-business-investigation`,title:`What Is a Restaurant Business Investigation?`},{category:`lease-assignments`,slug:`restaurant-lease-assignment-guide`,title:`Restaurant Lease Assignment Guide`}]},kd={slug:`how-to-sell-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Sell a Restaurant in Los Angeles`,seoTitle:`How to Sell a Restaurant in Los Angeles — Owner's Guide`,description:`How to sell your restaurant in Los Angeles: valuation, confidential listing, buyer qualification, escrow, license transfer, and closing. Written for LA owners.`,keywords:[`sell my restaurant`,`how to sell a restaurant`,`sell a restaurant in Los Angeles`,`restaurant broker Los Angeles`,`restaurant exit planning`],updatedAt:`2026-07-24`,readMinutes:10,keyTakeaways:[`Most independent LA restaurants sell for 1.5–3× seller's discretionary earnings, adjusted for lease and license.`,`Clean, reconciled books are the single biggest lever on final price.`,`Confidential listings protect your staff, vendors, and customers during the sale.`,`The lease and the ABC license drive buyer interest more than décor or concept.`,`A serious buyer is pre-qualified financially, on landlord standards, and on ABC eligibility — before you sign an LOI.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`Get your numbers ready`},{type:`p`,text:`Buyers pay for verified cash flow, not stories. Before you list, reconcile 24 months of POS reports, bank deposits, merchant statements, and sales-tax filings so a serious buyer can trace revenue in under an hour. Owners who can't produce these lose 20–40% of their theoretical price during due diligence.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Understand what your restaurant is worth`},{type:`p`,text:`In LA County, independent restaurants typically sell on a multiple of Seller's Discretionary Earnings (SDE): revenue minus real operating costs, added back with the owner's compensation and one-time expenses. The multiple depends on lease term remaining, license type, neighborhood, and how much of the operation depends on the owner personally.`},{type:`h3`,text:`What moves the multiple up`},{type:`ul`,items:[`5+ years remaining on the lease (with options).`,`Type 47 ABC license or a rare CUB overlay.`,`Documented, reconciled financials.`,`A trained GM who stays post-sale.`,`Strong second-generation build-out (hood, grease, plumbing already in place).`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Fix the deal-killers before you list`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Close out any open Health Dept violations or red tags.`,`Get unpermitted construction inspected or brought current.`,`Update the ABC file — clear any pending accusations.`,`Confirm equipment ownership; pay off leased items you'll represent as owned.`,`Get a landlord estoppel and read your assignment clause before a buyer does.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`List confidentially`},{type:`p`,text:`Public listings tell your staff, vendors, competitors, and regulars that you're leaving — before you have a buyer. A professional restaurant broker markets under a blind profile (neighborhood, cuisine, revenue range) and only reveals identity after a buyer signs an NDA and financial qualification.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Qualify buyers before the tour`},{type:`p`,text:`Every buyer inquiry should clear three gates before they walk your dining room: proof of funds or lender pre-approval, hospitality experience the landlord will accept, and no ABC disqualifiers (felony history, prior license discipline). This filter saves you weeks and protects the confidentiality of your listing.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Negotiate the LOI`},{type:`p`,text:`A strong LOI locks in price, deposit size, due-diligence window, lease-assignment contingency, ABC transfer terms, and closing conditions. The deposit should hit an independent escrow, not the buyer's attorney's trust account.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Escrow, transfers, and closing`},{type:`p`,text:`The buyer opens a California bulk-sale escrow, publishes the Bulk Sale Notice, and runs due diligence. In parallel, the ABC license transfer and Health Dept change of ownership run on their own clocks. Expect 60–120 days from accepted LOI to keys.`},{type:`h2`,text:`After the close`},{type:`p`,text:`Most LA restaurant sales include 1–4 weeks of seller training. Structure it in writing — hours per day, coverage of key relationships (vendors, landlord, health inspector), and any earn-out or seller-note terms.`}],faqs:[{q:`How long does it take to sell a restaurant in Los Angeles?`,a:`From listing to keys, plan on 4–9 months. The listing and buyer-search phase averages 60–120 days for a well-priced, well-documented restaurant; escrow and license transfers add another 60–120.`},{q:`What is my restaurant worth?`,a:`In LA, independent restaurants typically sell at 1.5–3× Seller's Discretionary Earnings, adjusted for lease term, license type, and neighborhood. A no-cost valuation call gets you a defensible range.`},{q:`Can I sell my restaurant without owning the building?`,a:`Yes — the vast majority of LA restaurant sales are asset sales, where the buyer takes an assignment of your lease. The building owner has to consent to the assignment, which is why the lease review happens early.`},{q:`Do I have to tell my employees I'm selling?`,a:`Not until you have a signed LOI and confirmed the buyer's plan for the staff. Confidential listings exist for exactly this reason.`},{q:`What are the tax implications of selling a restaurant?`,a:`Asset sales, stock sales, and installment sales are taxed very differently in California. Involve your CPA before you accept an LOI — allocation of purchase price to equipment vs. goodwill materially changes your tax bill.`}],related:[{category:`buying-a-restaurant`,slug:`how-to-buy-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles`},{category:`lease-assignments`,slug:`restaurant-lease-assignment-guide`,title:`Restaurant Lease Assignment Guide`},{category:`alcohol-licensing`,slug:`abc-license-transfer-guide`,title:`ABC License Transfer Guide`}]},Ad={slug:`restaurant-lease-assignment-guide`,title:`Restaurant Lease Assignment Guide (Los Angeles)`,seoTitle:`Restaurant Lease Assignment Guide — LA County`,description:`How restaurant lease assignments work in Los Angeles: landlord consent, guaranty structure, rent bumps, and negotiation tactics that get deals closed.`,keywords:[`restaurant lease assignment`,`restaurant lease transfer`,`commercial restaurant lease`,`restaurant lease negotiation`,`restaurant tenant representation`],updatedAt:`2026-07-24`,readMinutes:7,keyTakeaways:[`Almost every LA restaurant lease requires landlord consent to assign.`,`Landlords use assignment consent as leverage to raise rent, extend term, or tighten guaranty.`,`Start the landlord conversation in week one of escrow — waiting kills deals.`,`A well-drafted LOI makes landlord consent an explicit contingency.`,`Rent abatement during permit build-out is negotiable and often granted.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`What a lease assignment actually is`},{type:`p`,text:`An assignment transfers the existing lease from the seller (assignor) to the buyer (assignee). The lease terms usually stay the same — rent, term, use clause, options — but the tenant on the paper changes. Assignments are different from a sublease (where the seller stays on the hook) and different from a brand-new lease (where you negotiate everything from scratch).`},{type:`h2`,text:`Why landlords slow assignments down`},{type:`p`,text:`Consent gives the landlord a rare chance to reprice or reshape the lease mid-term. Expect requests for a rent increase, an extended term, a stronger personal guaranty, additional security deposit, or a share of the sale proceeds ('recapture' or 'profit participation'). Every one of these is negotiable.`},{type:`h2`,text:`The assignment package`},{type:`p`,text:`Landlords will ask for a package on the assignee. A tight package speeds consent:`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Personal financial statement and last two years of tax returns.`,`Two years of business tax returns for the operating entity.`,`Résumés showing hospitality experience.`,`Business plan or concept summary.`,`Proof of funds for purchase price plus 90-day working capital.`,`Bank and trade references.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Guaranty structures to negotiate`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Good-guy guaranty — personal liability ends when you return the space in agreed condition.`,`Burn-off guaranty — personal exposure decreases over time as you pay rent on time.`,`Capped guaranty — total personal exposure is capped at a dollar amount or number of months.`,`Full-recourse guaranty — you're personally on the hook for the full term. Avoid when possible.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Rent abatement during build-out`},{type:`p`,text:`If you're renovating, permitting, or waiting on ABC, negotiate abated or reduced rent tied to real milestones — Health Dept permit issuance, ABC license issuance, or doors-open day. Landlords grant abatement more readily than a permanent rent reduction because the loss is defined.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Consent conditions to watch`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Automatic recapture of options.`,`Waiver of the tenant's right to further assign.`,`Requirement that the seller-tenant remain secondarily liable.`,`Landlord approval of any future menu, hours, or concept change.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can a landlord refuse to consent to a restaurant lease assignment?`,a:`It depends on the lease. Most California commercial leases say consent 'shall not be unreasonably withheld.' A financially qualified, experienced operator is generally reasonable; a first-time buyer with weak reserves is where landlords push back hardest.`},{q:`How long does landlord consent take?`,a:`Two to eight weeks is typical. Institutional landlords with committee approval processes take longer; owner-operators can consent in days if they know the buyer.`},{q:`Do I have to pay the landlord an assignment fee?`,a:`Sometimes. Leases often carry an assignment fee ($1,000–$5,000) plus reimbursement of the landlord's legal review. Anything beyond that is negotiation.`},{q:`Can I get rent abatement even if the lease doesn't mention it?`,a:`Yes. Abatement is granted in the assignment consent document, not the original lease. If you're bringing a qualified tenant to a landlord who wants the deal done, abatement tied to permit milestones is one of the most successful asks we make.`}],related:[{category:`buying-a-restaurant`,slug:`how-to-buy-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles`},{category:`lease-assignments`,slug:`restaurant-lease-assignment-guide`,title:`Restaurant Lease Assignment Guide`},{category:`restaurant-leasing`,slug:`restaurant-leasing-overview`,title:`Restaurant Leasing in Los Angeles`}]},jd={slug:`abc-license-transfer-guide`,title:`ABC License Transfer Guide (California)`,seoTitle:`ABC License Transfer Guide — California Restaurant Buyers`,description:`How the California ABC license transfer works: person-to-person transfers, timelines, temporary permits, CUB conditions, and what triggers escrow release.`,keywords:[`ABC license transfer`,`liquor license transfer LA`,`California ABC person to person transfer`,`restaurant liquor license`,`type 47 transfer`],updatedAt:`2026-07-24`,readMinutes:6,keyTakeaways:[`Person-to-person ABC transfers typically take 60–90 days from application to issuance.`,`A temporary retail permit lets the buyer operate during the transfer window.`,`The escrow can't release funds until the ABC formally approves the transfer.`,`CUB (Conditional Use Beverage) restrictions on the license transfer with it — read them before you buy.`,`Certain criminal history, prior license discipline, or fictitious ownership will disqualify a buyer.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`The three types of ABC transfers`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Person-to-person — the license moves from one owner to another at the same premises. Most restaurant sales.`,`Premises-to-premises — the same owner moves the license to a new address.`,`Double transfer — person and premises change together.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Timeline you should expect`},{type:`ol`,items:[`Application filed with the local ABC district office — day 0.`,`30-day posting period on the premises begins.`,`Investigator assigned; interviews owners, reviews financials, checks premises.`,`Any protests reviewed; CUB conditions confirmed.`,`Issuance — typically day 60–90.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`Temporary retail permit`},{type:`p`,text:`The buyer can apply for a temporary retail permit at the same time as the transfer. Cost is modest, timeline is fast (often within a week), and it lets you operate under your name during the transfer window. Almost every LA restaurant closing uses one.`},{type:`h2`,text:`What can block or slow the transfer`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Undisclosed CUB conditions that conflict with the buyer's intended operation.`,`Buyer criminal history — certain felonies are automatic disqualifiers.`,`Prior ABC license discipline against the buyer.`,`Protests from neighbors, PTA, or nearby churches (rare on person-to-person).`,`Seller tax liens or unpaid alcohol excise taxes.`,`Incomplete or inconsistent ownership disclosure — the ABC takes fictitious-ownership violations seriously.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`CUB conditions — read them`},{type:`p`,text:`A Conditional Use Beverage permit is a list of local restrictions attached to the license: hours of sale, whether entertainment is allowed, food-service-to-alcohol ratio, no karaoke, no happy hour, and so on. CUBs travel with the license. Before you close, pull the ABC file and read the full CUB — a restriction you didn't know about can gut your concept.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Escrow release`},{type:`p`,text:`California requires the license transfer to be complete (or in defined final steps) before escrow funds are released to the seller. This protects both sides — the seller doesn't lose the license without payment, the buyer doesn't pay for a license they don't own.`}],faqs:[{q:`How much does it cost to transfer an ABC license in California?`,a:`State ABC fees are modest (typically under $1,500). The bigger costs are legal or licensing consultant fees, plus any premium the seller charges for the license itself when scarcity applies (Type 47s in some LA neighborhoods trade at a premium).`},{q:`Can I operate the bar during the ABC transfer?`,a:`Yes, under a temporary retail permit issued to the buyer. It's active until the full transfer issues or expires.`},{q:`What is a Type 41 vs Type 47 license?`,a:`Type 41 is beer and wine only, on-premises consumption, at a bona fide eating place. Type 47 is beer, wine, and distilled spirits, on-premises, at a bona fide eating place. A Type 47 is more valuable and more scarce in most LA neighborhoods.`},{q:`Do I need a licensing consultant?`,a:`For a clean person-to-person transfer with no CUB complications, many buyers file themselves. For anything involving CUB modifications, new licenses, disciplinary history, or protests, a specialized consultant is worth the fee.`}],related:[{category:`alcohol-licensing`,slug:`abc-license-transfer-guide`,title:`ABC License Transfer Guide`},{category:`buying-a-restaurant`,slug:`how-to-buy-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles`},{category:`health-permitting`,slug:`restaurant-health-permit-transfer`,title:`Restaurant Health Permit Transfer`}]},Md={slug:`restaurant-broker-beverly-hills`,title:`Restaurant Broker in Beverly Hills`,seoTitle:`Restaurant Broker Beverly Hills | Penthouse Advisors`,description:`Restaurant brokerage in Beverly Hills: lease negotiation, ABC transfers, off-market listings, and confidential sales for one of LA's most competitive submarkets.`,keywords:[`restaurant broker Beverly Hills`,`Beverly Hills restaurant for sale`,`Beverly Hills restaurant lease`,`restaurant broker 90210`],updatedAt:`2026-07-24`,readMinutes:5,keyTakeaways:[`Beverly Hills restaurant rents typically run $8–$18 psf/month NNN on prime corridors.`,`Type 47 liquor licenses in 90210 command premiums well above LA County averages.`,`The Beverly Hills Planning Commission actively reviews new hours, entertainment, and outdoor dining.`,`Off-market opportunities dominate — very few Beverly Hills restaurants publicly list.`],sections:[{type:`h2`,text:`The Beverly Hills market`},{type:`p`,text:`Beverly Hills is one of the highest-priced and most tightly regulated restaurant markets in Los Angeles County. Rents on the Rodeo, Canon, and Beverly corridors reflect luxury retail demand. Landlords are selective, the Planning Commission is active, and the ABC treats new hours or entertainment as a full public process.`},{type:`h2`,text:`Where transactions actually happen`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The Golden Triangle (Rodeo / Canon / Beverly) — luxury operators only.`,`South Beverly Drive — neighborhood dining, higher deal velocity.`,`Beverly Boulevard corridor — mix of casual and fine dining.`,`Wilshire — larger footprints, hotel-adjacent concepts.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`What Beverly Hills buyers get wrong`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Underestimating the landlord's qualification bar — Beverly Hills landlords vet operators harder than most of LA.`,`Assuming a valid CUB from another city transfers — it doesn't; Beverly Hills CUBs are location-specific.`,`Ignoring valet, sign, and outdoor-dining approvals — each is a separate city process.`,`Not budgeting for the higher food-cost customer expectation.`]},{type:`h2`,text:`How Penthouse Advisors works in 90210`},{type:`p`,text:`We work every side of Beverly Hills transactions — representing sellers confidentially, sourcing off-market space for tenants, and pairing buyers with the qualified LA operators our landlords will actually approve. If you're considering an entry, an exit, or a lease in Beverly Hills, one call is the fastest way to see what's really available.`}],faqs:[{q:`What does a Beverly Hills restaurant lease cost?`,a:`Prime Beverly Hills restaurant rents run roughly $8–$18 per square foot per month NNN in 2026, with the Golden Triangle at the top of the range. Secondary streets and Beverly Boulevard trade lower.`},{q:`How much is a Type 47 liquor license in Beverly Hills?`,a:`License premiums in 90210 vary with scarcity but typically run well above the LA County average because the ABC has issued a limited number of Type 47s in the city and new-issuance is slow.`},{q:`Do I need Beverly Hills city approvals in addition to state ABC?`,a:`Yes. Beverly Hills reviews outdoor dining, entertainment, extended hours, and any exterior signage as separate city processes. Build these into your timeline.`}],related:[{category:`buying-a-restaurant`,slug:`how-to-buy-a-restaurant-in-los-angeles`,title:`How to Buy a Restaurant in Los Angeles`},{category:`restaurant-leasing`,slug:`restaurant-leasing-overview`,title:`Restaurant Leasing in Los Angeles`},{category:`alcohol-licensing`,slug:`abc-license-transfer-guide`,title:`ABC License Transfer Guide`}]};function Nd(e,t,n,r,i,a,o,s=[]){return{slug:e,title:t,seoTitle:n,description:r,intro:i,iconName:a,order:o,articles:s}}var $=[Nd(`buying-a-restaurant`,`Buying a Restaurant`,`Buying a Restaurant in Los Angeles — Guides & FAQs`,`Guides, checklists, and FAQs for buying a restaurant in Los Angeles County — from first offer to keys in hand.`,`Everything a first-time or repeat buyer needs to acquire a restaurant in Los Angeles: how the process works, what due diligence to run, how financing and ABC transfers fit together, and where deals commonly break.`,`ShoppingBag`,1,[Dd,Od]),Nd(`selling-a-restaurant`,`Selling a Restaurant`,`Selling a Restaurant in Los Angeles — Owner Guides`,`How to sell your restaurant in LA: valuation, confidential listing, buyer qualification, escrow, and closing.`,`For owners planning an exit — how valuation actually works in LA, how to prepare your books and lease for a serious buyer, and how a confidential listing protects your staff, vendors, and reputation while the sale runs.`,`Store`,2,[kd]),Nd(`restaurant-leasing`,`Restaurant Leasing`,`Restaurant Leasing in Los Angeles — Tenant & Landlord Guides`,`Restaurant leasing in Los Angeles: second-generation spaces, lease negotiation, rent abatement, and tenant representation.`,`Restaurant leasing is a specialty, not a corollary of retail leasing. These guides cover second-generation restaurant spaces, tenant representation, rent abatement tied to permit milestones, and the landlord-side view.`,`DoorOpen`,3),Nd(`alcohol-licensing`,`Alcohol Licensing`,`California ABC License Guides — Transfers, Type 41 & 47`,`California ABC licensing: transfers, Type 41, Type 47, CUB conditions, and what buyers and sellers need to know.`,`The California ABC is its own timeline inside every restaurant transaction. These guides explain person-to-person transfers, temporary retail permits, CUB conditions, and how license issues surface (and get resolved) in escrow.`,`Wine`,4,[jd]),Nd(`health-permitting`,`Health Permitting`,`LA County Health Permitting & Plan Check Guides`,`Los Angeles County Health Dept: permit transfers, plan check, red-tag removal, and reopening.`,`The LA County Department of Public Health governs every restaurant's ability to open its doors. These guides cover change-of-ownership permits, plan-check submittal, red-tag responses, and the fastest path back to service after a closure.`,`ShieldCheck`,5),Nd(`grandfather-protection`,`Grandfather Protection`,`Restaurant Grandfather Protection — LA County Guides`,`How grandfather protection works for older LA restaurant spaces — and what triggers full code compliance.`,`Older LA restaurant spaces often carry grandfathered exemptions from current code. These guides explain what triggers a loss of grandfather status, how the 90-day rule works, and how to protect the exemption during a sale or renovation.`,`Lock`,6),Nd(`lease-assignments`,`Lease Assignments & Abatement`,`Restaurant Lease Assignment & Rent Abatement Guides`,`Restaurant lease assignments, landlord consent negotiation, and rent abatement tied to permit milestones.`,`Almost every restaurant sale in LA turns on the lease assignment. These guides cover landlord consent strategy, guaranty structures, and how to negotiate abated rent while you build out or wait on permits.`,`FileSignature`,7,[Ad]),Nd(`business-investigations`,`Restaurant Business Investigations`,`Restaurant Business Investigation Guides — LA Due Diligence`,`Pre-purchase restaurant business investigations: what to check, what to pull, and what to walk away from.`,`A business investigation is the buyer's insurance policy. These guides show exactly what documents to pull, what public records to check, and how to interpret what you find before your deposit becomes non-refundable.`,`FileSearch`,8),Nd(`buyer-tenant-resolutions`,`Buyer / Tenant Resolutions`,`Stuck Restaurant Deal? Buyer & Tenant Resolution Guides`,`How to resolve stuck restaurant deals — landlord holds, escrow disputes, license snags, and permit denials.`,`Deals stall. Landlords stop responding, ABC investigators raise unexpected issues, Health plan check comes back with corrections. These guides show how experienced advisors diagnose and unstick each kind of blocker.`,`Handshake`,9),Nd(`off-market-sourcing`,`Off-Market Restaurant Sourcing`,`Off-Market Restaurant Sourcing — LA Door-Knocking Campaigns`,`How off-market restaurant sourcing works in LA: door-knocking campaigns, owner outreach, and pre-listing negotiations.`,`Most of LA's real restaurant inventory never hits a public listing. These guides explain how door-knocking campaigns actually work, when they're worth it, and how to structure an approach to an owner who isn't formally selling.`,`DoorOpen`,10),Nd(`e2-visa`,`E-2 Visa Restaurant Investments`,`E-2 Visa Restaurant Guides for European Entrepreneurs`,`Restaurant investments structured for the E-2 investor visa — for European entrepreneurs relocating to LA.`,`For European entrepreneurs using a restaurant purchase as an E-2 qualifying investment, these guides cover deal structure, minimum investment thresholds, source-of-funds documentation, and the LA acquisition process.`,`Plane`,11),Nd(`commercial-property-management`,`Commercial Property Management`,`Commercial Property Management Guides — LA Landlords`,`Commercial property management in LA County: tenant management, rent collection, maintenance, and repositioning.`,`For LA landlords and investors — how full-service commercial property management works, what a professional manager actually does day-to-day, and how repositioning turns a struggling asset into a performing one.`,`Building2`,12),Nd(`medical-dental-leasing`,`Medical & Dental Office Leasing`,`Medical & Dental Office Leasing in LA — Guides`,`Medical and dental office leasing across Los Angeles County: medical-use zoning, existing build-outs, and tenant representation.`,`Medical and dental office leasing is a specialty market with its own zoning, plumbing, and equipment requirements. These guides cover what to look for in a medical-use space and how to structure a lease that protects the practice.`,`Stethoscope`,13),Nd(`restaurant-consulting`,`Restaurant Consulting`,`Restaurant Consulting Guides — LA Operators`,`Restaurant consulting for LA operators: turnaround strategy, profitability, expansion planning, and operations.`,`Beyond brokerage, these guides address the advisory side — turnaround strategy for underperforming restaurants, profitability levers, and how to plan a second location without losing the first.`,`Search`,14),Nd(`local-markets`,`LA Local Market Guides`,`Restaurant Broker Guides by LA Neighborhood`,`Restaurant brokerage market guides by Los Angeles neighborhood: Beverly Hills, Santa Monica, Pasadena, and more.`,`LA is not one restaurant market — it's dozens. These neighborhood guides cover rent ranges, landlord expectations, license scarcity, and city-specific approvals across the submarkets we work in most.`,`MapPin`,15,[Md])];function Pd(e){return $.find(t=>t.slug===e)}function Fd(e,t){let n=Pd(e);if(!n)return;let r=n.articles.find(e=>e.slug===t);if(r)return{article:r,category:n}}function Id(){return $.flatMap(e=>e.articles.map(t=>({category:e,article:t})))}var Ld=D(`/help/$category/`)({loader:({params:e})=>{let t=Pd(e.category);if(!t)throw xe();return{category:t}},head:({loaderData:e,params:t})=>{let n=e?.category,r=`/help/${t.category}`;return n?{meta:[{title:n.seoTitle},{name:`description`,content:n.description},{property:`og:title`,content:n.seoTitle},{property:`og:description`,content:n.description},{property:`og:url`,content:`${Fe}${r}`},{property:`og:type`,content:`website`},{name:`twitter:card`,content:`summary_large_image`}],links:[{rel:`canonical`,href:`${Fe}${r}`}],scripts:[Ne([{name:`Home`,path:`/`},{name:`Help Center`,pat